---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Drommejournal from '../../components/Drommejournal.astro';
import { alternatesFor } from '../../lib/statiske-alternates';
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  tittel="Dream journal"
  beskrivelse="A simple dream journal to write in each morning. Note the dream, tag the symbols and watch the patterns emerge. Everything stays in your own browser."
  alternates={alternatesFor('journal')}
>
  <div class="journal-side">
    <span class="static-label">Tool</span>
    <h1 class="static-title">Dream journal</h1>

    <div class="prose journal-intro">
      <p>
        What you remember of a dream disappears quickly. The first few minutes after waking are
        usually the only ones you get — by breakfast there is rarely more than a mood left. That
        is why a journal is the simplest and most effective thing you can do if you want to
        understand your dreams better: not because writing interprets anything by itself, but
        because it gives you something to interpret.
      </p>
      <p>
        What makes a journal worth the effort is not the individual dream. It is what comes back.
        A symbol you dismissed as coincidence the first time turns out to have appeared eight
        times in two months. A feeling you associate with nightmares turns out to belong to an
        entirely different kind of dream. Patterns that are invisible night by night become
        obvious once they sit underneath one another.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Drommejournal lang="en" />

    <div class="prose journal-rad">
      <h2>Getting the most out of the journal</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Write before you do anything else.</strong> Don't check your phone, don't get up.
        Dream recall fades fast from the moment you start engaging with the day. If all you have
        is three words, write the three.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Write in the present tense.</strong> "I'm standing in a hallway and the door is
        locked" pulls up more than "I dreamt I was standing in a hallway". The present tense keeps
        you inside the scene, and the details come more easily.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Record the feeling, not just the events.</strong> Two people can dream almost the
        same sequence of events and wake for completely different reasons. How you feel on waking
        is often the best clue to what the dream was about — and it is also the first thing you
        forget.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Tag the symbols, but don't interpret straight away.</strong> The tagging exists so
        that you can count later. Interpretation can wait until you have fifteen entries and can
        actually see what repeats.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Don't write every night.</strong> Most people who try to journal daily give up
        within a fortnight. Three mornings a week for six months will tell you far more than
        fourteen days in a row.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy and storage</h2>
      <p>
        The journal lives entirely in your browser, in something called localStorage. Nothing is
        sent to us, nothing is stored on a server, and we have no way of reading what you write.
        That also means the journal belongs to this browser on this device: it won't follow you to
        your phone, and it disappears if you clear your browsing data or use private browsing.
        That is what the download button is for — use it now and then.
      </p>

      <h2>Read on</h2>
      <p>
        If you want to go deeper into the method, we have a full guide to
        <a href="/en/guides/dream-journal/">keeping a dream journal</a>, covering setup, common
        pitfalls and what a journal can show you over time. If you struggle to remember your
        dreams at all, <a href="/en/guides/what-are-dreams/">what dreams are</a> is a good place
        to start. And if the same dream keeps returning,
        <a href="/en/guides/recurring-dreams/">recurring dreams</a> was written for exactly that.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .journal-side {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  .static-label {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-nav);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 1.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .static-title {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: 36px;
    color: var(--color-text-heading);
    margin-bottom: 24px;
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .static-title { font-size: 44px; }
  }
  .journal-intro { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .journal-rad { margin-top: 3rem; }
  .journal-rad h2 { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
</style>
